        <div id="three-kinds" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Three Kinds of Countertop Filter, and the One This Guide Leaves Out
          </h2>

          <p>
            Countertop water filter covers several quite different machines, and the right one depends on your faucet, your counter and whether you want filtered water when the power or the pressure is off.
          </p>

          <p>
            Gravity filters are two stacked chambers, usually stainless steel. You pour tap water into the top, it trickles through filter elements into the bottom tank, and you draw it from a spigot. There is no connection to the plumbing and no power. They hold two to three gallons, filter a few gallons an hour, and have become the default answer for people who want a large stock of filtered water and do not want to touch the sink. Berkey made the category famous; Culligan, Waterdrop and others now sell close copies that take the same size of filter.
          </p>

          <p>
            Faucet-diverter units are a filter canister on the counter, connected by a short hose to a diverter valve that replaces your faucet's aerator. Flip the valve and water runs through the canister and out of its own spout. They filter at tap pressure, so they are much faster than gravity, and their cartridges are larger than a faucet-mount filter's. They need a faucet with a standard threaded aerator, just like a <a href="/best-faucet-water-filter/">faucet-mount water filter</a>, which rules out most pull-down sprayers.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plug-in dispensers are the third type: a countertop appliance you fill by hand, which pushes water through a carbon cartridge with a pump and dispenses it at the press of a button. The Aquasana Clean Water Machine is one. They are faster than gravity and need no faucet connection, at the cost of a power socket.
          </p>

          <p>
            This guide leaves out countertop reverse osmosis units on purpose. They are a different technology for a different problem, removing dissolved solids, fluoride and nitrate, with a waste-water tank and a much higher price. If that is what your water report calls for, our <a href="/best-reverse-osmosis-water-filter-pitcher/">reverse osmosis water filter pitcher</a> guide covers the plug-in RO units properly. For whole-house problems, start with the <a href="/best-water-filtration-system-for-home/">best water filtration system for home</a> guide.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="certified-vs-tested" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Certified vs Lab-Tested: The Distinction That Matters Most
          </h2>

          <p>
            Gravity filters are where the gap between marketing and certification is widest. Many listings say a filter removes 99.9% of hundreds of contaminants, was tested to NSF standards, or exceeds NSF/ANSI 53. None of those phrases means the filter is certified.
          </p>

          <p>
            A certification means an accredited body such as NSF, IAPMO R&amp;T or the Water Quality Association tested production units against a named NSF/ANSI standard, audits the factory, retests periodically, and lists the product in a public directory. The maker then publishes a performance data sheet with the reduction for each substance. A lab test means the maker paid a lab to run some tests, often to its own chosen protocol, once. The results may be real and even impressive, but nobody is checking that the filter you buy matches the one that was tested.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then check which standard is certified. NSF/ANSI 42 covers chlorine taste and odor and particulates, and almost any carbon passes it. NSF/ANSI 53 covers health effects: lead, mercury, VOCs, cysts and PFAS. NSF/ANSI 401 covers emerging contaminants such as microplastics and trace pharmaceuticals. NSF/ANSI 372 only certifies that the device's materials are lead-free. A gravity filter certified to 42 and 372 is certified for chlorine taste and lead-free parts, and nothing more, even if its title says it reduces lead.
          </p>

