        <div id="berkey" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. The Berkey Question: EPA Orders and What Ships Now
          </h2>

          <p>
            Anyone shopping for a gravity filter runs into Berkey, and into a confusing story about why it is hard to find. Here is what the public record shows.
          </p>

          <p>
            Starting in December 2022 and through 2023, the EPA issued stop sale, use or removal orders under FIFRA, the federal pesticide law, to Berkey's maker and to several of its distributors. EPA's position was that Black Berkey elements contain silver to inhibit bacterial growth and were sold with antimicrobial claims, which makes them a pesticide that must be registered. Berkey's maker sued EPA in 2023, and according to Berkey's own support page, its request for an immediate injunction lifting the order was denied in January 2024. Berkey's site has since said Black Berkey elements are unavailable or in limited supply.
          </p>

          <p>
            What Berkey sells now is the Big Berkey system with Phoenix Gravity New Millennium Edition elements, made for Berkey's maker as a replacement for Black Berkey elements. Berkey says they are certified to NSF/ANSI 42 for chlorine, chloramine and particulates, and NSF/ANSI/CAN 372 for lead-free materials, without naming the certifying body on that page. It says the elements were tested by accredited third-party labs to EPA guidelines and NSF protocols and are designed to reduce VOCs, PFAS, pesticides, pharmaceuticals and some heavy metals, but those reductions are not covered by the certifications it lists.
          </p>

          <p>
            Older Big Berkey listings that still show Black Berkey BB9-2 elements turn up on Amazon. Given the stop-sale history, we recommend the Phoenix version, which is what Berkey itself currently sells, and we would be wary of any listing that cannot tell you which elements are in the box. Berkey's pages also state that its systems are not available to residents of California and Iowa.
          </p>

          <p>
            None of this means a Berkey does not filter water well. It means you are buying it on the maker's lab data, not on an independent certification for health contaminants. If you want the Berkey format with a health-effects certification, the Culligan MaxClear filters are sold as fitting Berkey chambers, which is the most practical Berkey water filter alternative we found.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="capacity-flow" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Capacity and Flow: Gallons per Hour Matter More Than Gallons in the Tank
          </h2>

          <p>
            Gravity filters are sold on tank size: 2.25 gallons, 3 gallons, 6 gallons. Tank size tells you how much filtered water you can store. It does not tell you how fast you get it, and that is the number that decides whether a gravity filter keeps up with a family.
          </p>

          <p>
            Berkey is unusually clear about this. It rates the Big Berkey with two Black Berkey elements at up to 3.5 gallons per hour, and with four at up to 7 gallons per hour, measured with the top chamber full, and says flow falls as the water level drops. It rates the Phoenix elements at up to 4.2 gallons per hour per pair. In practice that means refilling the top chamber once or twice a day for a household that drinks and cooks from it.
          </p>

          <p>
            The certified numbers are slower. Culligan's MaxClear performance data sheet gives a service flow rate of 4.0 gallons per day, the rate at which its certification testing was run. That is not a hard ceiling on what comes out of the spigot, but it is the flow at which the 99% lead and PFAS reductions were verified. It is worth knowing that slower flow through carbon means more contact time, and more contact time generally means better adsorption.
          </p>

          <p>
            The diverter and plug-in units play a different game. The Waterdrop CTF-01 is rated for a maximum of 1.6 gallons per minute at tap pressure, which fills a pot in a minute. The Aquasana Clean Water Machine is rated at 0.5 gallons per minute through its cartridge, and Aquasana says it pours an 8-ounce glass in 8 seconds, but its reservoir holds only 64 ounces, so you refill it often.
          </p>

