        <div id="filter-life" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Filter Life and Cost per Gallon: Where the Big Numbers Come From
          </h2>

          <p>
            Gravity filters advertise enormous filter lives: thousands of gallons per pair. Set those numbers next to certifications and a pattern appears. The longer the claimed life, the narrower the certified claim tends to be.
          </p>

          <p>
            Culligan rates a single MaxClear filter at 50 gallons, two at 100 and three at 150, and says to change them at that point. That sounds short until you see what it is certified to do across those gallons: 99.2% and 99.1% lead reduction, 99.6% total PFAS, 99.4% chlorine, 99.6% chloramine and 96.5% microplastics. Aquasana rates the Clean Water Machine cartridge at 300 gallons or six months, certified to NSF/ANSI 42, 53 and 401.
          </p>

          <p>
            Berkey rates each Phoenix element at 2,750 gallons, or 5,500 for a pair. Waterdrop's pages give up to 6,000 gallons for the TK-A's carbon filter pair and 8,000 gallons or 12 months for the CTF-01. Those are long lives, but the certifications behind them are NSF/ANSI 42 for chlorine and 372 for materials. Chlorine is the easy contaminant: carbon keeps reducing it long after it has stopped capturing the harder ones. A health-effects certification has to hold up to the end of the rated life, which is why certified capacities are shorter.
          </p>

          <p>
            To compare running costs, divide the replacement filter price by its rated gallons. As an illustration, a $50 pair of filters rated for 100 gallons is 50 cents a gallon; a $100 pair rated for 5,500 gallons is under 2 cents. Those are not like-for-like numbers, because one is buying certified lead and PFAS reduction and the other certified chlorine reduction. Decide what you need removed first, then compare cost among the filters certified for it.
          </p>

