        <div id="gravity-vs-diverter" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Gravity or Faucet-Diverter: Which Suits Your Kitchen
          </h2>

          <p>
            Choose a gravity filter if your faucet is a pull-down sprayer, if you want filtered water stored and ready, or if you want water when the taps stop. Gravity units connect to nothing, so faucet style is irrelevant, and a renter can take one to the next apartment without a wrench. The cost is counter space and height: Berkey lists the Big Berkey at 19.25 inches tall and 8.5 inches across in use, and on a stand it will not fit under most wall cabinets. Measure the gap before you buy, and remember you need room above it to pour into the top chamber.
          </p>

          <p>
            Choose a faucet-diverter unit if your faucet has a standard aerator and you want filtered water at something close to tap speed without plumbing. The Waterdrop CTF-01 filters at up to 1.6 gpm, so filling a pot is as quick as it is from the faucet. It takes a few inches of counter beside the sink, and the hose to the diverter is always in view. It shares the faucet-mount's fit limits: Waterdrop's listings exclude pull-out, handheld, spray-style and sensor faucets.
          </p>

          <p>
            Choose a plug-in dispenser if you want certified filtration, a small footprint and a fast glass of water, and you do not mind refilling a small reservoir. The Aquasana takes up about as much room as a coffee maker.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your faucet will take a faucet-mount filter and you only need drinking water, a <a href="/best-faucet-water-filter/">faucet water filter</a> is cheaper than any of these and takes no counter space at all. If it will not and you would rather not see a filter at all, an <a href="/best-under-sink-water-filter/">under sink water filter</a> is the plumbed route. A pitcher, from our <a href="/best-water-filter-pitcher/">best water filter pitcher</a> guide, is the cheapest of all if you only need a few glasses a day.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="emergencies" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Countertop Filters for Outages and Boil-Water Notices
          </h2>

          <p>
            One reason gravity filters sell so well is that they keep working when nothing else does. They need no power and no water pressure, so in an outage you can pour stored water through them, or water from a well with a failed pump. That is a real advantage over under-sink, faucet-mount and plug-in units, all of which stop when the pressure or the power goes. If you are planning for outages more broadly, pair one with the preparations in our <a href="/best-home-generator-for-power-outages/">home generator for power outages</a> guide and a way to hear official notices, such as an <a href="/best-emergency-radio/">emergency radio</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Be careful about what they are certified to handle in an emergency, though. None of the five filters in this guide carries a certification as a microbiological water purifier. Culligan's data sheet says its system is intended only for potable water and must not be used with water that is microbiologically unsafe or of unknown quality without disinfection before or after. Berkey's lab-tested claims about bacteria and viruses were exactly what put its Black Berkey elements on the wrong side of EPA, and the Phoenix elements are certified only to NSF/ANSI 42 and 372.
          </p>

          <p>
            So the honest rule is this. Under a boil-water notice, boil the water or follow the utility's instructions. A gravity filter can then improve taste and remove what its certifications cover. For untreated surface water, such as a creek after a storm, treat or boil first. A certified filter is not a substitute for disinfection.
          </p>

          <p>
            For storage, a 2.25- to 3-gallon gravity tank is a useful reserve but not a supply. Planning guidance commonly suggests about a gallon per person per day, so a family of four goes through a full Big Berkey tank in about half a day. Store water separately and use the gravity filter to make it pleasant to drink.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="care" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Setup and Care: Priming, Cleaning, Stainless vs Plastic
          </h2>

          <p>
            Gravity elements need some care to keep their flow. Berkey ships its Phoenix elements with a primer and red food coloring: priming forces water through the element to flush air and carbon fines, and the dye test confirms the element is seated and sealing, because unfiltered water bypassing a loose element is the most common gravity-filter failure. Culligan says MaxClear filters need no priming and do not need to stay wet between uses, and that the outer surface can be cleaned with a sponge to restore flow.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clean the chambers every few weeks with mild soap and water, and keep the unit out of direct sunlight, which Culligan's manual specifically warns against because it encourages algae growth in the bottom chamber. Culligan rates the MaxClear for water between 40 and 85°F. Do not put hot water in a gravity filter.
          </p>

          <p>
            Stainless or plastic is mostly about durability and taste. The Culligan, Berkey and Waterdrop TK-A chambers are stainless steel, which does not stain, does not hold odors and survives being dropped. The Aquasana dispenser and most diverter units are largely plastic. That is not a safety issue for a certified unit; NSF/ANSI 372 and the materials sections of the performance standards cover what touches the water. Culligan gives a five-year warranty on its Scout and Venture stainless systems, excluding the filters.
          </p>

          <p>
            Diverter units need the same care as faucet filters: flush a new cartridge before drinking, flip the diverter to unfiltered before running hot water, and check the adapter washer if it drips. Change cartridges at the rated gallons or calendar limit, whichever comes first, and sooner if flow falls off.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying on a contaminant count</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Listings that say a gravity filter removes 200 or more contaminants are quoting lab tests, not certifications. Find the certifier's name and the NSF/ANSI standard on a performance data sheet. On this list, only the Culligan MaxClear and the Aquasana publish health-effects certifications to NSF/ANSI 53 and 401.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Reading NSF/ANSI 372 as lead removal</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Several gravity filters advertise NSF/ANSI 42 and 372 certification next to a lead claim. Standard 372 certifies that the device's own materials are lead-free; it is not a lead-reduction certification. For lead in your water, look for NSF/ANSI 53 and a published lead percentage.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating a gravity filter as a purifier</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                None of these five is certified as a microbiological purifier. Culligan's data sheet says not to use its system on microbiologically unsafe water without disinfection. Under a boil-water notice, boil first. For creek or rain water, disinfect before or after filtering.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting the height</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A Big Berkey stands 19.25 inches tall in use, and you need room above it to pour in water. On a stand it will not fit under most wall cabinets. Measure the space, including pouring clearance, before buying any gravity unit, and plan where it will live permanently.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a Berkey listing without checking the elements</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Some Amazon listings still show Black Berkey elements, which have been under an EPA stop-sale order. Berkey's current system ships with Phoenix Gravity elements. Confirm which elements are in the box, and remember Berkey says its systems are not sold to California or Iowa residents.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
