        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best countertop water filter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For certified performance, the Culligan MaxClear Venture. Its filters are certified by IAPMO R&amp;T to NSF/ANSI 42, 53 and 401, with 99.2% lead and 99.6% total PFAS reduction on the data sheet. The trade-off is a 50-gallon rating per filter. For a plug-in alternative with the same three standards, the Aquasana Clean Water Machine is certified by WQA.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are gravity water filters NSF certified?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Some are, most are not for health contaminants. Many gravity elements, including Berkey's Phoenix elements and Waterdrop's TK-A filters, are certified only to NSF/ANSI 42 for chlorine and 372 for lead-free materials. Culligan's MaxClear filters are certified to NSF/ANSI 42, 53 and 401. Always check the standard numbers, not just the NSF badge.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What happened to Berkey water filters?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                From December 2022, the EPA issued stop-sale orders under FIFRA against Berkey's maker and distributors, treating Black Berkey elements as unregistered pesticides because of their silver and antimicrobial claims. Berkey's maker sued, and its request for an injunction was denied in January 2024. Berkey now sells systems with Phoenix Gravity elements, certified to NSF/ANSI 42 and 372.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is a good Berkey water filter alternative?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The Culligan MaxClear is the strongest one we found: a nesting stainless gravity system whose filters are certified to NSF/ANSI 42, 53 and 401, and which Culligan says fit Berkey chambers too. For a cheaper stainless tank with chlorine-only certification, the Waterdrop TK-A takes Berkey-size filters and is certified to NSF/ANSI 42 and 372.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do countertop water filters remove fluoride?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not the carbon units in this guide; none of them claims a certified fluoride reduction. Berkey and Waterdrop sell add-on fluoride filters for their gravity systems, but these are lab-tested claims. Countertop reverse osmosis is the reliable no-plumbing option for fluoride, and we cover those units in a separate guide.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How fast does a gravity water filter work?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Slowly. Berkey rates the Big Berkey at up to 3.5 gallons per hour with two Black Berkey elements and up to 4.2 per hour with Phoenix elements, both with the top chamber full and slowing as it empties. Culligan's certified service flow is 4.0 gallons per day. Adding more elements, where the chamber allows, speeds it up.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a countertop water filter work with a pull-down faucet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A gravity filter or a plug-in dispenser will, because neither connects to the faucet. A faucet-diverter countertop unit such as the Waterdrop CTF-01 will not: its diverter replaces the aerator, and Waterdrop's listings exclude pull-out, handheld, spray-style and sensor faucets. Unscrew your aerator first to see if your faucet takes one.
              </p>
            </div>

